# Per-repo exemptions for test/conventions.t: one path glob per line.
#
# THE BAR IS HIGH ON PURPOSE. A glob here drops the named path from ALL of
# that file's rules, not from the one it offends, so an exemption to protect
# two lines can silence eight checks over a whole file. Prefer reshaping the
# file; reach for this only where the format itself makes a rule impossible.

# share/config.sample documents every $MUX_DIR/config knob with its DEFAULT,
# and two of those defaults (the ssh templates for `latch-transport` and
# `desktop-notifier-transport`) are 118 and 121 characters. They cannot be
# wrapped: mux's config parser does NOT join a trailing backslash, it takes
# it literally, which this package has already paid for once when a wrapped
# `latch-transport` silently became `ssh -t ... \` with no host and broke
# `mux latch` on both machines. So a default shown across two lines is a
# default that does not work when copied, which is the one thing a sample
# exists to avoid.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT COST, because the honest version of an exemption is
# naming what replaces it: `test/mux-config-sample.t` asserts the rules that
# matter for this file (no em-dashes or double hyphens, every directive line
# inside the format, no TABs) AND the ones only it can ask, that the sample
# documents exactly the keys the source reads, in both directions. The
# generic checker is removed; coverage is not.
share/config.sample
